Here's a Python function to determine whether a given number is prime:

```python
def is_prime(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return False
    for I in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):
        if n % I == 0:
            return False
    return True
```

This function takes an integer `n` as input and returns `True` if `n` is prime and `False` otherwise. The function first checks if `n` is less than or equal to 1, since 1 and 0 are not prime. Then, it iterates through all integers from 2 up to the square root of `n` (rounded up), checking if `n` is divisible by any of those numbers. If `n` is divisible by any number in that range, it is not prime and the function returns `False`. If the loop completes without finding any divisors, `n` is prime and the function returns `True`.

To generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document, you can use the following commands:

1. Add the `toc` package to your document preamble:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage{toc}
   % other packages and settings
   \begin{document}
   ```

2. Use the `tableofcontents` command after your document's main title:

   ```latex
   \begin{document}
   \title{My Document}
   \maketitle
   \tableofcontents
   % body of your document
   \end{document}
   ```

3. Use the `\section`, `\subsection`, `\subsubsection`, etc. Commands to create the document's headings.

   ```latex
   \section{Introduction}
   \subsection{Background}
   \subsubsection{History}
   % body of your section
   ```

4. LaTeX will automatically generate the TOC based on the headings you've defined. The TOC will be displayed after the main title, and it will contain links to each section and subsection.

Here's a complete example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{toc}

\begin{document}
\title{My Document}
\maketitle

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Background}
\subsubsection{History}

This is the body of the Introduction section.

\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Data Collection}
\subsubsection{Sample Size}

This is the body of the Methodology section.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Descriptive Statistics}
\subsubsection{Mean and Standard Deviation}

This is the body of the Results section.

\section{Discussion}
\subsection{Implications}
\subsubsection{Limitations}

This is the body of the Discussion section.

\end{document}
```

When you compile the document, you should see a table of contents like this:

```
